findings.finding:
The response schema accepts many combinations the gate can never produce. Each of these frames passes check_frame:
- DELIVERED_RESULT with httpStatus 500, 408 or 302;
- WRITE_BEGIN maintenance OPEN with gateState ACTIVE or HELD, HELD with gateState CLOSED, PENDING with gateState CLOSED;
- MAINTENANCE_STATUS maintenance OPEN with inFlightActionId 1, with a non-empty ambiguousWriteIds, or with gateState ACTIVE;
- ambiguousWriteIds [5] with writes 0;
- WRITE_END ENDED with gateState ACTIVE.
Only G05 (an ambiguous response carrying a status) and G06 (WRITE_BEGIN reporting NONE) are pinned. W02b's status schema forbids the analogous impossible states with conditional rules. Here, the frames that tell the writer it may write (maintenance OPEN) are the least constrained. The gate enforces on its own state, so this misleads and nothing is forwarded, but Q4 asks for impossible combinations to be forbidden.

findings.requiredChange:
Add conditional constraints with frame vectors:
- maintenance OPEN => gateState in {CLOSED, INVALIDATED}, inFlightActionId null (status) and ambiguousWriteIds empty;
- maintenance HELD <=> gateState HELD when maintenance is PENDING, OPEN or HELD;
- maintenance other than NONE => gateState not ACTIVE or INSPECTING;
- DELIVERED_RESULT httpStatus in {200, 201, 202} or 400-499 except 408.
Or list each accepted but unreachable combination in the README, as the W02b round-3 review did.

questionAnswers.Q2:
Within the model's abstraction, no interleaving produced any of the following:
- a write forwarded while an admitted action lacked a durable terminal classification;
- a write outside OPEN or after the maintenance ended;
- a write without a durable WRITE_FORWARDED;
- an A1 CONSUMED record after MAINT_REQUESTED;
- a false classification (DELIVERED_RESULT only for a final status with a durable WRITE_TERMINAL DELIVERED_RESULT);
- a maintenance resumed after a restart;
- a write by a non-owner session.
Evidence:
- 600,000 randomized events (6,000 runs, scenario prefixes for in-flight and urgent-close starts). 1,845 runs reached MAINT_OPEN, 1,801 writes were forwarded, with 7,457 restarts, 15,099 storage failures, 4,716 writer peer changes and 17,684 writer session losses. 0 oracle violations.
- Storage failure injected at every record ordinal of all 45 transcripts, single and paired: 732 executed runs, 0 issues.
- 9 of 10 seeded mutants were caught, including the P1 drain-reply-as-grant mutant (64/400). The effect-kind mutant is caught by K28-K31.
- P1 probes: an urgent close with an action in flight (T04, PF, PG, PH), a drain pending before an urgent close (T05, PJ) and a failed seal (T06) all keep maintenance PENDING or HELD until the fence holds.
- Counterexample 18: T02, T03 and PL.
What remains:
- R1: after two unrecorded failures and a restart, a fresh maintenance can write despite an acknowledged HELD.
- R4: the outstanding-write window is not modelled.
- R7: deniedActionIds.

questionAnswers.Q4:
Envelope and bounds are sound:
- every one of the eight variants is closed (additionalProperties false, required lists, consts);
- strings are patterned and bounded, integers bounded, arrays and objects capped;
- check_frame enforces depth at most 16 and size at most 16 KiB, and decode_frame canonical, duplicate-free UTF-8.
The object-value rules (printable-ASCII strings and member names, integers within ±(2^53−1)) keep the canonical form equal to RFC 8785. An independent JCS serializer agreed on 20,000 random values. Every one of 6,683 gate responses and 7,337 writer requests re-decoded byte-exactly. -0, floats, NaN, non-ASCII, control characters and 2^53 are refused (G11-G15, B02-B09).
Response variants forbid only some impossible combinations (R3). Member-name bound off by one: R8.

questionAnswers.Q6:
No overclaim of installation, selection, custody, A2 objects, P1-P8 fixes or E01-E12 proof:
- the README says DATA_CHECK_ONLY, nothing installed, E01-E12 OPEN_UNPROVEN;
- the HTTP/TLS client, journal format, native custody, A2 names and P2-P8 are listed as open;
- the I05-side P1 bookkeeping is explicitly carried to W02b-F, with T05 pinning drainPending.
Two statements go further than the evidence:
- Decision 7's 'holds for I07 writes in every state' is true only within the atomic-write abstraction (R4);
- Channel item 2's HELD for a writer peer deviation is not durable under W02b P4 (R1).

reviewLimit:
This is a read-only, source-level review by an agent separate from the author. No repository file was created, modified or deleted. Probe scripts and outputs exist only in the reviewer scratchpad (w02c-review-probes/). No pytest, scripts/validate_*.py, runner, native, cloud, network or GitHub action was taken.

The soundness conclusions hold only within the reference model's abstraction:
- a write's upstream exchange is atomic inside WRITE_OBJECT (R4);
- journals are in-memory lists with one-shot failures, and a failed write leaves no trace (R1);
- writer custody is a W_PEER_CHANGED event;
- there is no real socket, TLS, HTTP, fsync, clock or apiserver;
- the model covers one generation.
The randomized campaign is not exhaustive, and the failpoint campaign covers transcript-derived sequences only. Kubernetes semantics were not re-checked against a pinned upstream tag, and the distribution is unselected.

This verdict is not an installed gate or writer, native qualification, a selected HTTP/TLS client, the A2 policy objects, a fix of W02b P1-P8, or authorization for anything installed. All E01-E12 remain OPEN_UNPROVEN. R1-R4 (MINOR) and R5-R8 (NOTE) may be carried, but each must be fixed or explicitly carried in the W02c status record and the next revision.
